WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.915
Hello everyone, in this video we'll introduce CAPABLE: Capability-Aware Policy Adaptation via Behavioral Latent Encoding.

2
00:00:08.057 --> 00:00:11.444
Consider a robot instructed to place the plate inside the drawer.

3
00:00:11.444 --> 00:00:16.679
A failure in one joint changes the robot’s physical capability, making the intended motion difficult to execute.

4
00:00:16.679 --> 00:00:19.143
The frozen OpenVLA-OFT policy continues producing nominal actions,

5
00:00:19.143 --> 00:00:22.838
unaware that the commanded motion no longer matches the robot’s actual response.

6
00:00:22.838 --> 00:00:25.302
Consequently, the robot deviates from the desired trajectory.

7
00:00:25.302 --> 00:00:30.537
CAPABLE addresses this problem by inferring the robot’s current capability from its joint-execution history and kinematic structure.

8
00:00:30.537 --> 00:00:34.848
A residual policy then adapts the frozen VLA action to the robot’s altered dynamics.

9
00:00:34.848 --> 00:00:37.619
The system receives the visual observation and language instruction.

10
00:00:37.619 --> 00:00:42.854
The frozen OpenVLA-OFT backbone processes these inputs and generates the nominal action chunk for the current phase.

11
00:00:42.854 --> 00:00:46.858
In parallel, CAPABLE maintains a sixteen-step history of commanded and observed joint motion.

12
00:00:46.858 --> 00:00:52.093
This history reveals discrepancies between what each joint was asked to do and what it physically achieved.

13
00:00:52.093 --> 00:00:54.556
A shared temporal encoder processes every joint independently.

14
00:00:54.556 --> 00:01:00.099
Sharing the encoder across joints avoids fixed joint identities and supports generalization to failures affecting previously unseen actuators.

15
00:01:00.099 --> 00:01:03.486
The resulting joint representations are combined with the current physical query,

16
00:01:03.486 --> 00:01:06.258
including the robot configuration, Jacobian, and nominal VLA action.

17
00:01:06.258 --> 00:01:09.029
Cross-joint attention then models how individual joint capabilities interact.

18
00:01:09.029 --> 00:01:14.572
These representations are aggregated into a capability latent describing the robot’s current ability to execute the requested motion.

19
00:01:14.572 --> 00:01:17.344
This latent conditions the residual actor through feature-wise modulation.

20
00:01:17.344 --> 00:01:21.655
The actor samples a residual correction, which is added to the nominal OpenVLA action.

21
00:01:21.655 --> 00:01:23.194
The corrected action is executed,

22
00:01:23.194 --> 00:01:27.506
and the new joint response is returned as feedback for continuous online capability estimation.

23
00:01:27.506 --> 00:01:31.817
CAPABLE therefore enables a frozen VLA policy to adapt online to unseen actuator failures—

24
00:01:31.817 --> 00:01:34.280
without fault labels, joint identifiers, or reference trajectories.

25
00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:36.128
The next slide compares three conditions.

26
00:01:36.128 --> 00:01:41.671
The left panel shows the healthy VLA, the middle panel the faulty VLA, and the right panel CAPABLE.

27
00:01:41.671 --> 00:01:44.442
We evaluated 28 tasks across all four LIBERO suites.

28
00:01:44.442 --> 00:01:49.677
CAPABLE adapts the action to recover tasks in which the faulty VLA deviates from the intended motion.

29
00:01:49.677 --> 00:01:52.449
The final slide shows CAPABLE on a Franka Panda.

30
00:01:52.449 --> 00:01:55.220
We first fine-tune the VLA and train the residual

31
00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:58.300
policy in a digital twin matched to the physical workspace.

32
00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:02.303
We then transfer both frozen policies to the real robot without hardware training,

33
00:02:02.303 --> 00:02:05.998
where CAPABLE adapts the motion under joint faults and completes the task.

34
00:02:05.998 --> 00:02:07.230
Thank you for watching.
